# Noma — Knowledge for the Agentic Era

A slide deck is just another block. Every slide has a stable ID, so an agent
can patch one bullet without touching the rest of the deck, and the same
source renders as a web deck (press **Present**), Markdown, LLM context, or a
PaperDOM canvas document (`noma render examples/deck.noma --to paperdom`).

::deck{id="pitch" title="Noma pitch" aspect="16:9" accent="#b9522a"}
:::slide{id="pitch-cover" layout="title"}
## The wiki your agents can safely edit {id="pitch-cover-title"}

Plain-text source · stable block IDs · proof before every write
:::

:::slide{id="pitch-problem" title="Knowledge bases were built for humans only"}
- Notion and Confluence store pages as opaque rich-text trees
- Agents rewrite whole pages, so every AI edit is a review nightmare
- Nobody can tell which paragraph is stale, cited, or approved

::::notes
Open with the pain: an agent "helpfully" rewrote the onboarding page and
nobody noticed the broken runbook link for two weeks.
::::
:::

:::slide{id="pitch-model" title="One source, composed like Lego" layout="two-column"}
::::card{title="Blocks" class="tone-info filled"}
Typed blocks — claim, decision, risk, card, slide — each with a stable ID.
::::

::::card{title="Tokens" class="tone-accent outline"}
A closed style vocabulary (`tone-*`, `elevated`, `span-2`) instead of free CSS.
::::
:::

:::slide{id="pitch-loop" title="Agents propose, humans approve"}
| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| Scope | Agent reads only the blocks it needs |
| Proof | Patch is simulated, validated, and hashed |
| Approve | A person accepts the diff |
| Apply | Hash-checked write to the exact block |
:::

:::slide{id="pitch-statement" layout="statement" class="tone-accent"}
Every edit is a reviewable diff against a stable block.
:::

:::slide{id="pitch-quote" layout="quote"}
"We stopped asking whether the wiki is current — the stale-knowledge sweep tells us."
:::
::

## How to author a deck

::grid{columns=3 compact}
:::card{title="Deck" class="elevated"}
`::deck{aspect="16:9" title="..."}` wraps the slides.
:::

:::card{title="Slide" class="elevated"}
`:::slide{layout="title|section|content|two-column|statement|quote|media|blank"}`
:::

:::card{title="Notes" class="elevated"}
`::::notes` inside a slide holds speaker notes; they never appear on the slide.
:::
::
